  "notes": "The warm, unhurried traditionalist. Harmony is stated against the format: \"his productions carry ii–V–I turnarounds and secondary dominants more than the radio norm, and the Don Williams lineage means the tonic is chosen for warmth rather than brightness,\" so ii–V–I leads and the seventh share runs high. The kit is the clearest set of negatives in this batch and each is authored as one: \"warm and largely uncompressed; brushes and cross-stick on the ballads, a light two-beat on uptempo cuts, kick on 1 and 3, and **jazz-derived rim-and-tom fills rather than rock crashes**. Low velocity, low ornament\" — so there is no crash lane at all, the accent tier is the quietest of the producers here, and the fill grid is long. The bass is the other real delta: \"live bass with real voice-leading — inversions and passing tones rather than root-fifth; upright on the torch material,\" which is `independent_riff` at a low root-following probability with the widest passing-tone set here. Rubato is stated as tolerated — \"back-phrased entries and notes held across chord changes\" — hence the low quantize strength and the legato articulation. The Sugarland restraint is the arrangement note: \"drumless verses and voice-plus-one-instrument openings.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: \"roster and lane documented; technical method not reachable [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the humanize values, the velocity tiers and the counter ratio.",
